  "id": "COMPOUND:43014 - PROTEIN:6231",
  "verbalization": "Harmalacidine is implicated in the regulation of Histone-lysine N-methyltransferase, H3 lysine-9 specific 5, through a multi-step pathway involving the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This complex is targeted by multiple compounds, including (+)-Phylliroside, quillaic acid, and pioglitazone, all of which are known to interact with histone methyltransferases. The evidence suggests that Harmalacidine, through its interaction with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, may exert a modulatory effect on the activity of this enzyme. The presence of multiple compounds that target the same complex and ultimately lead to the same downstream effect supports the hypothesis that Harmalacidine could influence the enzyme's function. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Harmalacidine to act as a modulator of histone methyltransferase activity, possibly through the regulation of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This mechanism underscores the importance of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex in the broader context of epigenetic regulation and its potential therapeutic relevance.",
